# Backend Rules (FastAPI + SQLAlchemy)

## Layering

- `routers/` are thin: parse input via Pydantic schemas, call a service, return a schema.
- `services/` hold business rules (targeting, conflicts, status transitions). They take a DB
  session and plain arguments, and are unit-testable without HTTP.
- `models/` hold ORM models only. `schemas/` hold request and response models only.
- Never return ORM objects directly; always use response schemas (no password hashes, no
  internal fields).
- Constants (roles, statuses, limits, page sizes, time windows) live in `constants.py`.

## Validation and errors

- Every request body has a Pydantic schema with explicit constraints: max lengths, allowed
  enums, date ranges (`end > start`), and sensible defaults. Trim strings.
- Reject bad input with 422 and readable messages; never let a missing optional field crash
  a handler (robustness is a graded non-functional requirement).
- One consistent error shape: `{"error": {"code": "...", "message": "..."}}`. Use correct
  status codes (400, 401, 403, 404, 409 for conflicts, 422).
- Register global exception handlers. Log unexpected errors server-side, return a generic
  message to the client, never a stack trace.

## Auth and permissions

- Password hashing with argon2 or bcrypt. No plaintext or reversible storage.
- Token-based auth (short-lived signed token). Secret key from environment.
- All mutating endpoints require a permission check using the central permission mapping
  in `project-context.mdc`. Ownership checks (own society, own faculty, own post) are in the
  service layer, not the UI.
- Deny by default: a new endpoint without an explicit permission is a bug.
- Write an `AuditLog` row for sensitive actions (publish emergency, role changes, booking
  approval, content deletion).
- Rate-limit login and the assistant endpoint (simple in-memory limiter is fine).
- Privacy: return only the personal data a caller needs. Do not expose student emails or
  profiles to other students.

## Data and performance

- Indexes on foreign keys and on columns used in filters (`type`, `status`, `starts_at`,
  `expires_at`, audience fields, resource + time range).
- All list endpoints are paginated (`page`, `page_size`, capped by a constant) and support
  the relevant filters and search.
- Audience targeting rule: a post with a null audience field applies to everyone for that
  field; a student sees a post if every non-null audience field matches their profile.
- Content lifecycle: `DRAFT`, `PUBLISHED`, `ARCHIVED`; hide expired posts by default, but keep
  them for staff. Pinned and emergency posts sort first.
- Store timestamps in UTC; the UI renders in Asia/Colombo.

## Booking rules

- Overlap test: `new.start < existing.end AND new.end > existing.start`, against bookings that
  are `PENDING` or `APPROVED` for the same resource. Return 409 on conflict.
- Do the conflict check and insert in one transaction.
- Enforce bounds: minimum/maximum duration, opening hours, no bookings in the past,
  maximum advance window (all constants).
- Status flow: `PENDING -> APPROVED | REJECTED | CANCELLED`. Validate transitions in the service.

## Requests and listings

- `Request.status`: `OPEN -> IN_PROGRESS -> RESOLVED | CLOSED`. Only handlers with the right
  role may change status or respond.
- `Listing.status`: `ACTIVE -> RESOLVED | REMOVED` (e.g. item returned, book taken).
  Contact between users happens through the platform; do not publish phone numbers by default.

## Testing (pytest)

- Use an in-memory or temporary SQLite database per test session with the seed fixtures.
- Minimum coverage: permission denials (403) per role, audience targeting, booking conflict,
  status transitions, validation failures, assistant scope and fallback paths.
- Tests must run with one command (`make test` or `pytest`) and pass before every push.

## Config, logging, docs

- Settings via `pydantic-settings` from environment; `.env.example` lists every variable
  without real secrets.
- Use the `logging` module (no `print`). Never log passwords, tokens, or full personal data.
- Public functions and modules get short docstrings explaining the why.
- Expose `/health` (liveness) and `/ready` (DB reachable, assistant configured or fallback mode).
- Keep FastAPI's auto docs (`/docs`) enabled; they double as API documentation in the report.
